// Each entry supplies a concrete trace, an answered follow-up, and a solved drill.
// These are authored per topic rather than category-wide completion prompts.
const notes = {
  'Variables and assignment': ['A name lets a program reuse a stored value. Compute the expression before storing its result.', 'declare count with 2 | compute count plus 3 | count now stores 5', 'Is = an equality check? No. = assigns; == compares primitive values. A final local can be assigned once, and reading an unassigned local is a compiler error.', 'Replace count + 3 with count * 3: output becomes 6. Printing count before and after assignment shows the change.'],
  'Classes and objects': ['An instance combines related state and behavior. Two instances of one class can hold different state.', 'create first and second Counter | increment only first | output is 1:0', 'Does assignment create another instance? No. Counter alias = first copies a reference. Use new Counter to construct a separate instance.', 'Increment second twice: output becomes 1:2. Making value static would incorrectly share state across instances.'],
  'Null and references': ['A reference supplies a way to reach an object. null names no object, so member access through null fails.', 'first and alias reach one Counter | alias sets value to 7 | clearing first leaves alias able to print 7', 'When is an object eligible for collection? Only after no live retaining path reaches it. Clearing one reference is insufficient while an alias remains.', 'Read first.value after clearing first: it throws. Reading alias.value still returns 7; assignment did not copy the Counter.'],
  'Build tools and dependencies': ['A repeatable build turns source and declared libraries into a tested artifact. Version declarations make inputs reviewable.', 'compile with javac into out | launch using out as class path | Maven automates stages from a POM', 'What does dependency scope change? It controls which build and runtime class paths include a library. A test dependency does not automatically belong in the production artifact.', 'Remove a required JAR from the class path: imports no longer resolve. Restore a declared compatible dependency rather than an undeclared local file.'],
  'SQL foundations': ['A table holds rows with named columns. A key identifies each row; a query selects rows and columns.', 'create learners with primary key id | insert learner 1 Ada | select id 1 returns Ada', 'Why not check uniqueness only before INSERT? Concurrent transactions can both pass that check. A database unique constraint enforces the invariant during writes.', 'Insert id 1 twice: the primary key rejects the duplicate. Query id 2: there is no row, which differs from a row containing NULL.'],
  'HTTP foundations': ['A client requests a representation; a server replies with a status and optional body. Method semantics guide caching and retries.', 'send GET learners 1 | authorize and locate learner | return 200 with Ada representation', 'Does a timeout mean the server did nothing? No. The response may be lost after processing. Writes need a safe retry or reconciliation policy.', 'Request a missing learner: return a documented 404. A network timeout instead supplies no HTTP response and does not establish the server outcome.'],

  'JDK vs JRE vs JVM': ['A source file is text; the compiler checks it and writes bytecode, which a compatible runtime executes.', 'Write Greeting.java | javac creates Greeting.class | java Greeting prints Hello from the JVM', 'Why can compilation succeed but launching fail? The launcher may use an older runtime or a different class path. Compare java -version, javac -version and the launch directory.', 'Change the greeting and rerun only java Greeting. It prints the old greeting until javac recompiles the changed source.'],
  'Primitive types': ['A type defines which values a variable can hold and which operations make sense. int stores whole numbers; boolean stores a decision.', 'count holds 2000000000 | cast count to long before multiplication | multiplying by 3 produces 6000000000', 'Does assigning an int product to long prevent overflow? No. Arithmetic happens before assignment; promote an operand first or use Math.multiplyExact when overflow should fail.', 'Remove the long cast: int multiplication wraps to 1705032704. Restore the cast and verify 6000000000.'],
  'Wrapper classes': ['A wrapper is an object representing a primitive value. Unboxing extracts its primitive; null means there is no object to extract from.', 'Integer value holds 42 | unbox into int answer | printing answer produces 42', 'Why avoid == for two Integers? It compares object identity; caching can make small values misleading. equals compares values, with an explicit null policy.', 'Uncomment unboxing of missing. It throws NullPointerException. Handle missing separately instead of silently treating it as zero.'],
  'Casting and promotion': ['A conversion changes the type used to represent a value. A wider range does not always mean exact precision: int to float can round.', 'byte operands are 100 and 27 | arithmetic promotes both to int | sum is 127 and narrowing still fits', 'Is every widening conversion lossless? No: int to float and long to float or double can lose low-order precision. Widening preserves range, not all representable integers.', 'Change right to 28. sum is 128 but narrowing to byte yields -128. Check bounds before narrowing when truncation is invalid.'],
  'Operators': ['An expression computes a value. && checks its right side only when its left side is true; this lets a guard avoid unsafe work.', 'active is true and quantity is 2 | multiply 50 by 2 then subtract 10 | allowed and positive total evaluates true', 'How do && and & differ for booleans? Both compute conjunction, but & evaluates both sides. A null check protects a dereference only when short-circuit evaluation skips it.', 'Set active to false. The final result is false even though total remains 90. Parentheses clarify arithmetic independently of authorization.'],
  'Control flow': ['if selects a path; a loop repeats a body. A loop counter must move toward a stopping condition or execution may never finish.', 'sum starts at 0 | add 2 then 4 then 6 | sum becomes 12 and the loop stops', 'When should I use while instead of for? Use while when repetition depends on a changing condition rather than a known count; still define what makes progress.', 'Run with zero iterations: sum remains 0. With three iterations, the running totals are 2, 6 and 12; this checks initialization and termination.'],
  'Methods and parameters': ['A method names reusable work. A parameter receives a copy of an argument value; return sends a result back to the caller.', 'caller passes 4 to twice | method computes 4 times 2 | caller prints returned value 8', 'Does reassigning a parameter change the caller variable? No. The parameter is a local copy. For objects, that copied reference can still point to the same mutable object.', 'Pass 0 and then -3 to twice. Results are 0 and -6. Changing the parameter inside the method does not reassign the caller variable.'],
  'Packages and imports': ['A package gives a class a qualified name. Imports let source code use shorter names; they do not install external libraries.', 'declare package com.example.orders | compile OrderService with -d out | launch com.example.orders.OrderService from class path out', 'Are com.example and com.example.orders the same access boundary? No. Subpackages are separate packages, so package-private members do not become visible through the naming hierarchy.', 'Remove the import and use java.util.List explicitly: type resolution is the same. A missing third-party JAR cannot be fixed by adding an import.'],
  'Access modifiers': ['Visibility controls which callers may use a member. A private field lets methods enforce rules before state changes.', 'Counter starts with private value 0 | increment changes it to 1 | public result prints 1', 'Does protected mean every subclass may access every instance field? No. Outside the package, protected instance access also restricts the qualifying reference type; it is not public access.', 'Try reading counter.value from an unrelated top-level class: compilation fails. Use increment to change state through the public contract.'],
  'Constructors': ['new allocates an object and invokes its constructor. Validation establishes the rules the instance must always satisfy, called invariants.', 'provide port 8080 | reject values outside 1 through 65535 | store and print valid port 8080', 'Does declaring a constructor retain an automatic no-argument constructor? No. Once a constructor is declared, add a no-argument constructor explicitly only if a valid default exists.', 'Construct ports 0 and 65536. Both must fail; 1 and 65535 must succeed. Checking only positivity misses the upper bound.'],
  'Inheritance': ['A subclass is a specialized form of a parent type. Overriding replaces an instance method while keeping the promise made to parent-type callers.', 'Account variable references SavingsAccount | call fee through Account | overridden method returns 0', 'How is overriding different from overloading? Overriding chooses instance behavior using runtime type. Overloading chooses a signature using compile-time argument types.', 'Instantiate Account instead of SavingsAccount: fee becomes 5. Keep the variable typed Account to observe runtime dispatch directly.'],
  'Composition': ['Composition gives an object a collaborator instead of making it a subclass. Delegating work separates responsibilities and permits replacement.', 'Checkout receives a ReceiptPrinter | checkout calls printer.print | printer supplies the receipt text', 'When is inheritance preferable? Use it for a genuine subtype whose observable contract remains valid. Use composition for replaceable implementation behavior without an is-a relationship.', 'Supply a printer that prefixes COPY. Checkout needs no code change; the collaborator determines the variation.'],
  'Abstract classes': ['An abstract class can share state and implemented methods while requiring subclasses to implement missing behavior. It cannot be instantiated directly.', 'Shape declares abstract area | Square stores side 3 | area returns 9', 'Why not always use an interface? An abstract class can centralize instance state and protected implementation, but consumes the single class-inheritance slot and couples subclasses to that base.', 'Try new Shape(): compilation fails. Add a concrete Square with side 0 and verify area is 0 before deciding whether zero is valid in your domain.'],
  'Interfaces': ['An interface names a capability that unrelated classes can implement. A caller depends on the capability rather than one concrete implementation.', 'Greeter declares greet | FriendlyGreeter implements greet | interface-typed caller receives Hello Ada', 'Can an interface contain implementation? Yes, default and static methods are allowed. It still has no per-instance fields and cannot replace a stateful base class in every design.', 'Add a second Greeter returning Welcome Ada. The caller can use it unchanged because both implementations satisfy the same signature and meaning.'],
  'Polymorphism': ['One declared type can refer to several implementations. An instance-method call selects the implementation belonging to the actual object.', 'Animal reference holds Dog | invoke sound | Dog override returns woof', 'Are static methods polymorphic? Static calls are resolved from the declared type; fields are also not dynamically dispatched. Use instance methods for replaceable behavior.', 'Replace Dog with Cat: sound changes to meow. An overload with a different parameter list would not demonstrate overriding.'],
  'Enums': ['An enum defines a fixed set of named values. It prevents misspelled strings from representing states that do not exist.', 'Status defines NEW PAID CANCELLED | select PAID | name prints PAID', 'Should an enum ordinal be persisted? Usually no: reordering constants changes ordinals. Persist a stable explicit code and define handling for unknown historical codes.', 'Add SHIPPED and revisit each switch. A default that silently treats new states as PAID can hide missing business behavior.'],
  'Records': ['A record is a compact data carrier whose components define accessors and value equality. A final reference does not make the referenced object immutable.', 'create Point with coordinates 3 and 4 | read both component accessors | their sum is 7', 'Are records deeply immutable? No. A List component can still be mutated unless defensively copied, and mutable components can destabilize equality or hashing.', 'Create two records with identical primitive components: equals is true. For a mutable collection component, make a defensive copy when snapshots are required.'],
  'Sealed classes': ['A sealed type deliberately limits its direct subtypes. This is useful when the application owns every alternative of a closed domain.', 'Result permits Success and Failure | Success carries a value | an unrelated direct implementation is rejected', 'Does sealing validate incoming data? No. It restricts Java type declarations; constructors and input boundaries must still validate payloads and nulls.', 'Try adding Other implements Result without changing permits. Compilation fails; adding an intended alternative requires updating the owned hierarchy and its consumers.'],
  'String immutability': ['A String stores text that cannot be edited in place. A variable can be reassigned, but that changes the reference rather than the existing text.', 'prefix is java | concat creates java-21 | prefix still prints java', 'Why can Strings be map keys? Their equality and hash remain stable. This does not make an unbounded map safe or guarantee cheap repeated concatenation.', 'Ignore the concat return value. prefix stays java. Assign the return value to a new variable to retain the combined text.'],
  'StringBuilder': ['A builder is a mutable text buffer. Append changes that buffer; toString returns a String snapshot for callers.', 'append 1 | append 2 and 3 | snapshot prints 123', 'Is sharing a builder safe? StringBuilder has no synchronization guarantee. Prefer one locally owned builder per operation; synchronization must cover the whole compound operation if shared.', 'Take a snapshot after appending 1, then append 2. The old String stays 1 while the builder now contains 12.'],
  'Arrays': ['An array groups a fixed number of elements under one reference. Its first index is zero, and length is one past the last valid index.', 'scores contains 7 8 9 | accumulate each element | print total 24', 'Does final int[] freeze its contents? No. final prevents reassigning the reference; elements remain mutable. Copy the array when ownership requires isolation.', 'Use an empty array: total is 0. Accessing scores[scores.length] throws because the last valid index is length minus one.'],
  'ArrayList': ['A List preserves positions and allows duplicates. ArrayList grows an internal array when additional storage is needed.', 'append Ada at index 0 | append Linus at index 1 | get 1 returns Linus', 'Why is append amortized constant time? Most appends fill a free slot; occasional growth copies existing elements. One resize is linear even though many appends average constant work.', 'Insert Grace at index 0. Ada moves to 1 and Linus to 2, demonstrating why middle or front insertion shifts elements.'],
  'LinkedList': ['A linked list stores elements in connected nodes. End operations avoid shifting an array, but indexed lookup walks nodes.', 'append first | append second | removeFirst returns first', 'Is insertion always faster than ArrayList? No. Finding an indexed position costs linear time and nodes add allocation and poor locality. Measure complete operations rather than only relinking.', 'Remove both values, then call pollFirst: it returns null. removeFirst on an empty deque throws instead.'],
  'HashSet': ['A set stores unique values. Hashing narrows candidate locations and equals decides whether two values count as the same.', 'add java | add equal java again | set size remains 1', 'Can two unequal values have the same hash? Yes. A collision requires equals checks; equal values must share a hash, but equal hashes do not prove equality.', 'Add Java with a capital J. Size becomes 2 because String equality is case-sensitive; normalize explicitly if the domain wants case-insensitive identity.'],
  'TreeSet': ['A sorted set keeps one representative for each comparison-equivalent value. Comparison, not insertion order, decides where elements belong.', 'insert 30 10 20 | tree orders values 10 20 30 | first and last produce 10-30', 'What if comparison returns zero for unequal objects? TreeSet treats them as the same element. Use tie-breakers when all distinct domain values must survive.', 'Insert 20 again: size stays 3. Query ceiling(15): it returns 20, an ordered operation a HashSet does not supply.'],
  'HashMap': ['A map associates keys with values. HashMap uses a key hash to locate candidates and equality to select the right mapping.', 'put u-7 with paid | hash and compare lookup key u-7 | get returns paid', 'Why are mutable keys dangerous? Changing a field used by hashCode after insertion can route later lookups to another bucket. Use stable identity fields or immutable keys.', 'Put u-7 with refunded. Size remains 1 and get returns refunded: a repeated key replaces its value rather than adding a duplicate entry.'],
  'TreeMap': ['A sorted map stores key-value pairs in key order. Floor means the greatest key no larger than the requested key.', 'put 10 low and 20 high | ask floorEntry 15 | select key 10 and print low', 'When should I prefer TreeMap? Use it when sorted iteration or range and neighbor queries are part of the contract. HashMap is usually simpler for equality lookup alone.', 'Ask floorEntry(5): the result is null, so getValue without an absence check would throw. Boundary queries need an explicit missing policy.'],
  'PriorityQueue': ['A priority queue repeatedly selects the smallest ordered value. Its internal heap only guarantees the root priority, not globally sorted iteration.', 'offer 30 10 20 | heap root is 10 | poll removes and prints 10', 'Does iteration visit priority order? No. Poll repeatedly to consume in order, or copy and sort when consumption must preserve the queue.', 'Poll three times: 10, 20, 30. A fourth poll returns null. Mutating an enqueued priority does not automatically repair heap order.'],
  'ArrayDeque': ['A deque supports both ends. Add at the tail and remove at the head for a queue; use one end for a stack.', 'append compile | prepend checkout | removeFirst returns checkout', 'Why reject null? poll uses null to signal emptiness, so a stored null would make that outcome ambiguous. Concurrent access also needs a different ownership policy.', 'Remove checkout and compile, then pollFirst. It returns null. removeFirst would throw on the same empty state.'],
  'equals and hashCode': ['Identity asks whether two references point to one object; equality asks whether two objects represent the same logical value.', 'store u-7 | create another String with content u-7 | contains returns true using value equality', 'Must unequal objects have different hashes? No. Only equal objects must have equal hashes. Hash collisions are legal and resolved using equality.', 'Compare the new String using == and equals: identity is false while value equality is true. Both values must produce the same hash.'],
  'Comparable and Comparator': ['Ordering compares two values and returns a negative, zero or positive result. Comparable belongs to the type; Comparator supplies an external policy.', 'names starts Bob Ada | both lengths are 3 | stable list sort keeps Bob Ada', 'Why avoid subtracting ints in compare? Subtraction can overflow and reverse ordering. Use Integer.compare and explicit tie-breakers for distinct values.', 'Sort by length then natural name: Ada precedes Bob. Equal-length input exposes why a secondary ordering rule may be needed.'],
  'Generics': ['A type parameter is a placeholder for a caller-selected type. Box<String> accepts text and returns text without a cast; Box<Integer> is a separate compile-time use.', 'create Box<String> | put java into its String slot | get returns java without a cast', 'Why is List<Integer> not List<Number>? If it were, callers could insert a Double into an Integer list. A producer wildcard permits safe reading without allowing arbitrary writes.', 'Try putting 42 into Box<String>. Compilation fails before execution. Object storage would instead require casts and permit mistakes until runtime.'],
  'Wildcards and PECS': ['A wildcard describes a family of generic types. Producer Extends, Consumer Super names the direction values can safely cross the API.', 'source holds integers 1 and 2 | read Number through producer bound | consumer accepts numbers and prints [1, 2]', 'Can I add an Integer to List<? extends Number>? No: the actual list might be List<Double>. Read as Number; use a consumer bound when adding integers is required.', 'Copy integers into a List<Number>. It is valid through ? super Integer; reading that consumer yields Object without a stronger type guarantee.'],
  'Type erasure': ['Java checks most generic constraints at compilation, then erases type arguments from ordinary runtime class identity. Compiler-inserted casts maintain typed use sites.', 'create ArrayList<String> | add Ada and get element zero | compiler checked retrieval prints Ada', 'Why can I not use new T()? Erasure leaves no concrete constructor target. Pass a factory or class token when runtime construction is a real requirement.', 'Compare the two getClass results: they match. This does not make the lists interchangeable at compile time; unchecked raw access can still corrupt their assumptions.'],
  'Checked exceptions': ['An exception reports a failure instead of returning a normal result. A checked exception requires a caller to catch it or declare propagation.', 'read config path | file operation may throw IOException | caller reports configuration unavailable', 'Should I catch Exception everywhere? No. Catch failures you can recover from or translate meaningfully, preserve the cause, and let the appropriate boundary decide the response.', 'Read a nonexistent temporary path: recovery runs. Read a known UTF-8 file: its content is returned. Test both paths without depending on a developer machine file.'],
  'Unchecked exceptions': ['Unchecked exceptions represent failures a method need not declare, often invalid arguments or violated assumptions. They still require intentional handling at application boundaries.', 'receive invalid argument | validation throws IllegalArgumentException | normal processing stops', 'Does unchecked mean safe to ignore? No. It changes compiler enforcement, not runtime impact. Validate inputs and map failures at a boundary without concealing programming errors.', 'Supply the smallest invalid value and assert the exception type. Then supply the smallest valid value to prove the guard is not off by one.'],
  'try-with-resources': ['Some objects own files, sockets or database handles. try-with-resources closes AutoCloseable resources when the block ends, including exceptional exits.', 'open resource | use it inside try | close it on both success and failure', 'What if use and close both throw? The body exception stays primary and close exceptions are suppressed. Inspect getSuppressed when cleanup failures matter to diagnosis.', 'Use a resource that records close calls and throw inside the block. close must still execute exactly once; this proves cleanup rather than only happy-path output.'],
  'Custom exceptions': ['A custom exception names a distinct failure that a caller can recognize. It should carry a stable meaning and retain the original cause when translating another failure.', 'detect domain-specific failure | construct exception with context and cause | boundary selects recovery or response', 'When is a custom type unnecessary? If callers cannot take a different action and an existing type expresses the contract, another class adds little value.', 'Wrap an IOException and check getCause returns that same object. Replacing it with message-only text would lose the diagnostic chain.'],
  'Functional interfaces': ['A functional interface supplies one abstract behavior. Predicate<T> asks a yes-or-no question about a T value.', 'nonBlank receives java | check not null and not empty | test returns true', 'Can it also have default methods? Yes. The functional constraint concerns abstract methods, excluding matching public Object methods; default and static helpers do not add abstract behaviors.', 'Test null, empty text and java. Results are false, false and true. The null guard prevents trim from dereferencing a missing value.'],
  'Lambda expressions': ['A lambda supplies behavior where a functional interface is expected. Its parameters and return type come from that target interface.', 'sort asks how to compare names | lambda compares ignoring case | list becomes Ada Linus', 'Why must captured locals be effectively final? The lambda captures their values rather than sharing an assignable local variable. A captured mutable object can still change and may introduce races.', 'Sort names in reverse comparison order: Linus comes first. Keep the comparison pure so repeated comparisons do not mutate the list.'],
  'Stream pipelines': ['A stream describes a traversal over data. Intermediate operations are lazy; a terminal operation starts traversal and produces a result or effect.', 'source amounts are 3 8 12 | keep amounts at least 8 | sum produces 20', 'Can I reuse a stream after sum? No. A terminal operation consumes the pipeline. Create a new stream from the source for another traversal.', 'Use an empty source: sum returns 0. Change the threshold to 13: no values pass and sum again returns 0.'],
  'Collectors': ['A collector combines stream elements into a result container, such as counts grouped by key. It defines how accumulation and combination work.', 'names are Ada ada Linus | normalize with Locale.ROOT | count ada twice and linus once', 'Why can toMap fail on duplicates? Without a merge function, duplicate keys are rejected. Decide whether duplicates mean combine, keep, replace or invalid input.', 'Collect an empty list: the result is empty. Use the same name with different capitalization and verify the explicit normalization policy groups them.'],
  'Optional': ['Optional represents either one present value or absence. It asks the caller to choose an absence policy instead of dereferencing null accidentally.', 'nickname is absent | ofNullable creates empty Optional | orElse selects anonymous', 'How do orElse and orElseGet differ? orElse evaluates its argument eagerly; orElseGet invokes the supplier only when empty. Use the latter for expensive fallback work.', 'Supply nickname Ada: label becomes Ada. Return Optional.empty for no result; returning null defeats the contract.'],
  'Regular expressions': ['A regular expression describes a pattern in text. Java string escaping happens before the regex engine interprets the pattern.', 'compile a bounded simple pattern | match a@example.com | whole-input matches returns true', 'Does this validate every real email address? No. It is a small syntax demonstration. Complex address policy and delivery verification need more than this regex.', 'Match text containing a space: this example rejects it. Test both ordinary spaces and backslashes to detect mistakes in Java double escaping.'],
  'Date and time API': ['Use a date for calendar-only meaning and an instant for a point on the global timeline. A zone supplies rules that map between local time and instants.', 'due is 2026-09-04 | plusDays 7 creates another LocalDate | next is 2026-09-11', 'Is a day always 24 hours? A calendar date increment is not a fixed elapsed duration across daylight-saving transitions. Select Period or Duration according to the business meaning.', 'Print due after plusDays: it remains 2026-09-04 because LocalDate is immutable. Test a month boundary such as January 31 plus one day.'],
  'Files and Paths': ['Path identifies a location; Files performs I/O. A path object does not guarantee that a file exists, is readable or stays unchanged.', 'create temporary file | write then read UTF-8 text | close handles and delete temporary file', 'Why not read every file with readString? It loads the whole content in memory. Bound file size or process a stream when input can be large.', 'Read a missing file and handle IOException. Use a temporary directory in tests so existing local files do not change the outcome.'],
  'Serialization boundaries': ['Serialization converts values to a transferable representation. Deserialization must treat incoming bytes as untrusted data and validate the resulting structure.', 'domain value enters boundary | encode a documented schema | validate version and fields when decoding', 'Why avoid native Java deserialization for untrusted input? It can instantiate object graphs with unsafe behaviors. Prefer a constrained schema and still enforce size, depth and authorization limits.', 'Remove a required identifier from an incoming payload. Reject it with a safe validation result; a successful parse alone does not prove a valid domain object.'],
  'Modules': ['A module groups packages and explicitly declares what it reads and exposes. Exported packages form a public boundary between named modules.', 'orders requires java.sql | exports only orders.api | another module cannot access an unexported implementation package', 'How are exports and opens different? exports permits ordinary access to public API; opens permits reflective access. Framework reflection may need narrowly scoped opens.', 'Move a public implementation type into an unexported package. A different named module cannot compile an ordinary reference to it even though the class is public.'],
  'Assertions': ['A Java assert checks an internal assumption when assertions are enabled. It must never be the only validation protecting user input or a required side effect.', 'execute with -ea | false internal condition is checked | AssertionError reports the broken assumption', 'Why not validate a request with assert? Assertions may be disabled. Use explicit validation and an exception or safe error response for external input.', 'Run the same program with and without -ea. Required behavior must remain correct in both modes; only internal diagnostic checks may differ.'],
  'JUnit test design': ['A test fixes an input, performs an action and checks an observable result. A useful regression fails for the defect it is intended to prevent.', 'arrange known inputs | invoke subject once | assert returned result and meaningful failure behavior', 'Why avoid sleeps in tests? Scheduler timing varies. Use deterministic synchronization or controlled clocks to test a specific transition instead of hoping enough time has elapsed.', 'For a sum method, assert 2 plus 3 equals 5 and 0 plus 0 equals 0. Deliberately replace addition with subtraction: the first test must fail.'],
  'Mocks and stubs': ['A stub supplies controlled responses. A mock additionally verifies selected interactions. Both replace a dependency, so neither proves that the real adapter works.', 'stub dependency response | run business decision | assert result before incidental call details', 'When do mocks make a test brittle? Verifying every internal call ties the test to implementation order. Verify interactions only when the interaction is part of the observable contract.', 'Make a stub return not-found, then success. Assert the service maps each result correctly; separately integration-test the real adapter.'],
  'Property-based testing': ['A property states a rule that should hold for many generated inputs. A generator explores cases and shrinking reduces failures to a smaller counterexample.', 'enumerate lengths zero through thirty | reverse each generated list twice | verify original sequence returns in all 31 cases', 'Does random input prove correctness? No. Generator coverage and the property itself can be weak. Keep reproducible seeds and add discovered counterexamples as regressions.', 'For sorting, check ordered output and equal input/output element counts. Checking order alone would wrongly accept an implementation that always returns an empty list.'],
  'Testcontainers': ['An integration test can start a disposable real dependency in a container. Readiness must be established before using the dynamically mapped connection details.', 'start pinned database image | wait for readiness and obtain mapped port | run adapter assertion then stop container', 'Does a container prove production behavior? It improves adapter realism but does not reproduce production topology, traffic or failover. Keep those validation boundaries explicit.', 'Insert a row and read it through the actual adapter; roll back a second transaction and verify no row is visible. A mocked repository cannot prove either SQL behavior.'],
  'Class loading': ['A class loader locates class definitions. Loading, linking and initialization are different stages; a class name alone does not uniquely identify a runtime type.', 'locate class bytes | link and verify definition | initialize static state before required active use', 'Can two same-named classes differ? Yes. Runtime type identity includes the defining loader. Plugin systems can therefore hit ClassCastException between identically named types.', 'Load a class without initialization using Class.forName with initialize=false. A static initializer should run only when initialization is later triggered.'],
  'Heap and stack': ['Each thread has call frames containing local execution state. Shared object storage is conceptually the heap; a reference is not the object itself.', 'method receives object reference | frame holds reference value | referenced object may be shared with another caller', 'Does every new object require a physical heap allocation? The JVM may optimize allocations when behavior is preserved. Use the conceptual model for correctness and evidence for performance claims.', 'Copy a reference and mutate the object through one alias. Both aliases observe the same object; assigning one alias to null does not clear the other.'],
  'Garbage collection': ['A collector reclaims heap storage for objects no longer reachable from live roots. Reachability is about references, not whether the application still finds the object useful.', 'allocate object | remove every live retaining path | collector may later reclaim storage', 'Does System.gc guarantee reclamation now? No. It is a request subject to runtime policy. Do not use it to implement resource cleanup or predictable application timing.', 'Keep an object in a static collection and drop its local reference. It remains reachable through the collection; removing that entry changes eligibility, not a guaranteed collection time.'],
  'Java Memory Management': ['Heap, thread stacks, class metadata and native allocations consume different budgets. Fix the exhausted region rather than treating every memory symptom as a heap leak.', 'allocate 1024-byte payload | requests retains payload | unbounded retained requests increase the live set', 'Can a container run out of memory with a stable heap? Yes. Thread stacks, direct buffers and other native memory also consume the process budget.', 'Remove retained payloads and compare post-GC live set under the same workload. Total allocations can stay high even when retention is fixed.'],
  'JIT compilation': ['The runtime can compile frequently executed bytecode into native instructions. Early execution and optimized steady state can therefore have different costs.', 'interpret or lightly compile method | observe hot execution profile | optimize while preserving Java behavior', 'Why is a single stopwatch run misleading? Warm-up, dead-code elimination and profile changes distort results. Use a harness such as JMH and report the workload and forks.', 'Make the benchmark consume its result and vary input. If unused work disappears, the faster time does not prove the intended computation became faster.'],
  'Memory leaks': ['A managed-memory leak is unwanted retention: reachable objects survive even though the application no longer needs them.', 'insert ten unique payload keys | remove the oldest key after size exceeds three | final retained map size is 3', 'How do I distinguish churn from a leak? High allocation alone is churn. Persistent growth of the post-GC live set plus a retaining path supports a retention diagnosis.', 'Replace an unbounded cache with a defined size/expiry policy and test repeated unique keys. Check retained size after steady load, not only startup memory.'],
  'JFR diagnostics': ['Java Flight Recorder collects runtime events over time. Correlating a bounded recording with a reproducible symptom is more useful than guessing from one aggregate metric.', 'observe checkout latency | record 60 seconds with profile settings | inspect allocation locks and CPU in that window', 'Are samples exact invocation counts? No. Sampling and event thresholds affect visibility. Absence of a sample does not prove an operation never happened.', 'Record a known workload before and after one change. Compare the same time window and traffic while protecting identifiers that custom events may contain.'],
  'Threads': ['A thread is an independently scheduled execution path. Starting work and waiting for it are separate operations; shared state needs coordination.', 'construct worker | start schedules independent run | join waits for worker completion', 'Does calling run start a thread? No. It is an ordinary call on the current thread. start creates the concurrent execution and can be called only once per Thread.', 'Record thread names in run, then compare direct run with start plus join. Verify completion through join rather than a sleep.'],
  'synchronized and locks': ['A lock lets one owner enter a protected region at a time. Every access participating in the invariant must use the same synchronization policy.', 'acquire shared lock | read and update protected state | release makes changes visible to a later acquirer', 'Does volatile replace a lock for count++? No. Increment is a read-modify-write sequence. Use a lock or atomic operation for a shared counter.', 'Run two workers performing a fixed number of increments, join both, and verify the exact total. Lock both readers and writers; a lucky unprotected run proves little.'],
  'Atomics and CAS': ['Compare-and-set changes a value only if it still equals an expected value. Atomic classes provide indivisible operations for suitable single-variable state.', 'read current counter | compare expected and update atomically | exactly one successful increment is recorded per call', 'Does an AtomicInteger make two fields consistent together? No. Separate atomic operations do not form one transaction. Protect the combined invariant with one state transition or lock.', 'Increment from two joined workers and verify the total. Also test the business bound separately; atomic increment alone does not enforce a maximum capacity.'],
  'Executors': ['An executor separates submitted work from worker management. Queue capacity and rejection determine what happens when work arrives faster than it can finish.', 'submit tasks | bounded workers take queued work | shutdown and await completion release lifecycle resources', 'Does a fixed thread pool guarantee bounded memory? Not necessarily: its queue can be unbounded. Bound admission as well as worker count and define a rejection policy.', 'Saturate a one-worker bounded pool with a blocked task and a full queue. The next submission must follow the documented rejection policy; release the worker and terminate cleanly.'],
  'CompletableFuture': ['A future represents a result that may arrive later. Composition describes dependent work without treating the future itself as the completed value.', 'start result-producing work | attach transformation | completion delivers transformed result or failure', 'Does cancelling a CompletableFuture stop its underlying operation? Not generally. Cancellation of the stage is not proof that an HTTP call or task was interrupted. Own resource cancellation separately.', 'Complete the source exceptionally and assert the dependent result fails. Also test success; joining only a successful path misses error propagation.'],
  'Virtual threads': ['Virtual threads are lightweight Java threads suited to many blocking tasks. They reduce thread-management cost but do not create database connections or CPU capacity.', 'start blocking tasks on virtual threads | waiting tasks yield carrier capacity when supported | join tasks and bound downstream use', 'Do virtual threads make CPU work faster? They do not add CPU cores. For blocking services, limit scarce dependencies and measure scheduling and runtime-version-specific pinning behavior.', 'Submit more requests than available database permits. Verify concurrent database access stays within the permit count even when many virtual threads exist.'],
  'JDBC basics': ['JDBC is Java’s standard database API. A connection owns session and transaction state; statements execute SQL and result sets expose rows.', 'obtain configured connection | execute query | consume rows and close result statement and connection', 'Does closing a pooled connection end the physical connection? Usually it returns the logical lease to the pool. Always close your lease and avoid retaining it across unrelated work.', 'Query an empty table: next returns false. Add one row and verify exactly one result, then check resources close on a SQL failure.'],
  'PreparedStatement': ['A prepared statement separates SQL structure from parameter values. Parameter binding prevents input values from becoming executable SQL syntax.', 'SQL contains question-mark placeholder | bind identifier as a value | database evaluates fixed query structure', 'Can I bind a table name with a placeholder? No. Placeholders represent values. Map allowed identifiers from trusted choices instead of concatenating arbitrary input.', 'Bind a value containing a quote and SQL-like text. It must be treated as one value, with no extra statement execution or changed predicate.'],
  'Transactions': ['A transaction groups related database operations under a commit or rollback boundary. Isolation determines what concurrent transactions may observe.', 'disable auto-commit | perform related writes | commit all or roll back local database changes', 'Can rollback undo a remote payment? No. A database transaction cannot atomically roll back an unrelated HTTP side effect. Use an explicit distributed recovery design.', 'Force the second write to fail and verify the first is absent after rollback. Also test success and restore connection state before returning a pooled lease.'],
  'Indexes and query plans': ['An index is an additional lookup structure that can reduce scanned rows. It costs space and maintenance on writes, so its value depends on the query shape.', 'query filters a key | planner estimates access choices | inspect actual rows and selected scan', 'Why might an index be ignored? A table scan can be cheaper for low-selectivity predicates or small tables; stale estimates and expressions can also change the plan.', 'Compare selective and broad predicates with EXPLAIN ANALYZE on test data. Report actual row counts and write cost rather than assuming every index helps.'],
  'SOLID principles': ['These design heuristics reduce the number of unrelated reasons a change can break code. A small stable contract is useful only when it separates real responsibilities.', 'OrderService needs notification | inject Notifier contract | replace delivery without changing order rules', 'Does SOLID require an interface for every class? No. Extra abstraction can increase coupling and navigation cost when there is no meaningful variation or boundary.', 'Replace the notifier with an in-memory fake. Order decisions should remain testable without a mail server, while a separate adapter test checks delivery integration.'],
  'Builder pattern': ['A builder collects named construction options and creates a result after validation. It helps when many optional constructor arguments would be confusing.', 'set title Daily | set limit 100 | build validates and returns final Report', 'Can I reuse a mutable builder safely? Only with a documented lifecycle and defensive copies in the result. Later builder edits must not silently alter an already built immutable object.', 'Build with a missing required title: reject it. Build two results from different settings and verify changing the builder does not mutate the first result.'],
  'Factory pattern': ['A factory chooses and constructs a product behind a common contract. The caller asks for a supported kind instead of constructing every concrete variant itself.', 'request upper formatter | factory selects Upper implementation | formatting java prints JAVA', 'How should unknown formats behave? Reject explicitly or return a documented absence. Silently selecting a default can parse the wrong format and hide configuration mistakes.', 'Ask for an unsupported format: it throws IllegalArgumentException. Request lower and verify JAVA becomes java; selection and formatting are separate responsibilities.'],
  'Strategy pattern': ['A strategy represents one replaceable algorithm under a common contract. The context delegates the decision rather than embedding every policy branch.', 'context receives discount | selected strategy applies seasonal rule | result reflects that policy', 'How is Strategy different from Factory? Strategy varies behavior during use; Factory varies product creation. A factory may select a strategy, but the responsibilities differ.', 'Apply no-discount and seasonal-discount to the same subtotal. Verify each result and explicit rounding policy; changing the policy must not change context code.'],
  'Observer pattern': ['A publisher notifies subscribed listeners about an event. You must decide whether listeners run synchronously and what happens when one fails.', 'register audit listener | publish OrderPlaced | listener records event under chosen delivery policy', 'Does an in-process observer guarantee durable delivery? No. A crash can lose notifications. Use a durable handoff when recovery across process failure is required.', 'Add a listener that throws and another that records. Test the chosen error-isolation policy and unsubscribe behavior rather than assuming every listener always runs.'],
  'Decorator pattern': ['A decorator wraps the same interface and adds behavior around delegation. Wrapper order determines which behavior observes or repeats other work.', 'client calls Auditing wrapper | audit records the attempt | delegate returns receipt 100', 'Is retrying a payment decorator safe by default? No. An ambiguous successful charge can be duplicated. Retry only with a verified idempotency and outcome policy.', 'Use a fake component that counts calls and throws. Assert wrapper ordering and propagation; enable retries only for an operation whose duplicate effects are controlled.'],
  'Dependency inversion': ['High-level business policy owns the contract it needs. Infrastructure implements that contract, letting the policy avoid depending directly on a vendor SDK.', 'Checkout calls owned PaymentPort | adapter implements that port | test substitutes a fake port', 'Is dependency injection the same principle? Injection supplies collaborators; inversion defines which side owns and depends on the abstraction. One mechanism does not automatically imply the other.', 'Replace the SDK adapter with a fake failure response. Checkout should map the failure using its own domain contract without importing vendor exception types.'],
  'Spring IoC': ['Inversion of Control means a container constructs and connects managed objects. A bean is simply an object whose lifecycle the container manages.', 'register service and repository | container resolves constructor dependency | obtain connected service bean', 'Does new Service use Spring injection? No. An object created outside the container is not automatically managed. Use explicit construction in unit tests or retrieve the managed bean in application code.', 'Remove a required collaborator registration: context startup should fail clearly. Supply a test implementation and verify the service behavior without changing its constructor.'],
  'Spring beans': ['Bean scope describes how the container creates and shares instances. The default singleton is one instance per container definition, not one per request.', 'register bean definition | container creates scoped instance | callers receive reference according to scope', 'Is a singleton bean thread-safe? Scope does not make mutable state safe. Keep request state local or give shared state an explicit concurrency policy.', 'Request a singleton twice and compare identity. Then send separate request values and verify they are not retained in shared instance fields.'],
  'Spring profiles': ['A profile selects groups of configuration for an environment. It changes which definitions participate; it is not an authorization boundary or secret store.', 'activate test profile | select test-specific configuration | context resolves test dependency', 'Can profiles replace validation? No. Missing or conflicting settings still need startup validation, and secrets should come from an appropriate protected configuration source.', 'Start with test and production configuration separately and assert the selected adapter. An unexpected profile combination must not silently connect a test to production.'],
  'Spring Boot configuration': ['Boot supplies conventions and conditional auto-configuration. External properties choose values, while typed binding and validation make configuration assumptions explicit.', 'load property sources | bind application settings | validate before using configured service', 'Why did my property value lose? Multiple sources have precedence. Inspect the effective configuration safely and identify the winning source without logging secrets.', 'Supply an invalid port or missing required URL. Assert startup validation fails instead of discovering the configuration problem on the first user request.'],
  'Spring MVC': ['MVC routes an HTTP request to a controller. The controller translates transport data, delegates business work and returns an HTTP representation.', 'request matches route | bind and validate input | service result becomes status and body', 'Should controllers hold business transactions? Keep business decisions and transaction boundaries in application services so non-HTTP callers can share the same behavior.', 'Test a valid request, malformed input and a missing resource. Assert status and safe response shape; do not rely only on verifying a service method was called.'],
  'Spring validation': ['Validation checks declared constraints on incoming data. Parsing success is separate from valid business meaning, and authorization is another independent check.', 'bind request fields | evaluate constraints | reject invalid input before service mutation', 'Does @Valid enforce every business invariant? No. Cross-record rules, uniqueness under races and access control need service and database enforcement appropriate to their consistency boundary.', 'Send blank required text and a boundary-sized value. Assert rejection has a safe field error and causes no database write.'],
  'Spring Security': ['Authentication identifies a principal; authorization decides whether that principal may perform an action on a particular resource.', 'request enters filter chain | establish identity | enforce operation and resource authorization', 'Is hiding a button authorization? No. Enforce access on the server and check resource ownership; a caller can construct the HTTP request directly.', 'Test anonymous, authenticated owner and authenticated non-owner requests. The non-owner must not receive protected data even with a valid login.'],
  'Spring Data JPA': ['A repository expresses persistence operations; JPA manages entities within a persistence context. Actual SQL and fetch behavior still determine performance and correctness.', 'repository loads aggregate | deliberate fetch plan loads required lines | map needed values before leaving boundary', 'Why can a loop produce N+1 queries? Accessing unloaded associations can trigger one extra query per entity. Inspect SQL and choose a bounded fetch plan or projection.', 'Load several orders and assert the query budget for required fields. Repeat with no children and multiple children to catch accidental row multiplication.'],
  'Testing Spring applications': ['Use the smallest test environment that proves the contract: plain unit tests for decisions, slices for framework boundaries, and full context for wiring.', 'select MVC slice | provide controlled collaborator | assert HTTP status and response', 'Does a passing mock test prove database integration? No. It proves behavior against the supplied fake contract. Use a real adapter test for SQL, mapping and transaction semantics.', 'Make the collaborator report not-found, invalid input and success. Assert each HTTP response, then separately exercise actual persistence with an integration fixture.'],
  'Actuator and observability': ['Metrics summarize behavior, logs describe events and traces connect work across boundaries. Operational endpoints must expose only intended information to authorized callers.', 'request carries context | record bounded telemetry | correlate symptom with diagnostic evidence', 'Should liveness check every dependency? Usually no: restarting a healthy process during a database outage can worsen the incident. Separate process health from readiness to serve.', 'Request management endpoints anonymously and with authorized access. Check intended exposure and ensure identifiers do not create unbounded metric labels or leak secrets.'],
  'Resilience patterns': ['Resilience defines how work fails and recovers under dependency trouble. Deadlines, retries and capacity controls must operate under the same resource budget.', 'start dependency operation | apply bounded deadline and admission | return known result or explicit failure', 'Can retries improve every failure? No. Permanent failures and overload often become worse; only retry classified transient failures when duplicate outcomes are controlled.', 'Inject a slow dependency and repeated failures. Assert bounded attempts and admitted work, then verify recovery without an unbounded backlog.'],
  'REST resource design': ['HTTP describes operations on resources using methods, status codes and representations. A stable URL alone does not define a complete API contract.', 'client addresses orders resource | server validates and authorizes | return documented status and representation', 'Are PUT and POST interchangeable? PUT requests replacement at a known target and is idempotent by HTTP semantics; POST delegates processing and needs an explicit retry policy.', 'Request a missing order and a forbidden order. Define safe 404 or 403 behavior without leaking another user’s resource details.'],
  'Idempotency': ['An operation is idempotent when repeating the same intent does not add another effect. A key must be bound to the caller and payload under a durable policy.', 'receive pay-7 | atomically claim intent and commit local result | replay stored receipt for matching retry', 'What if the payment provider succeeded but the response was lost? The outcome is ambiguous. Reconcile with provider identity; a local deduplication row alone cannot guarantee one external charge.', 'Retry the same key and payload, then the same key with changed amount. Return the original result for the first and reject the conflicting intent for the second.'],
  'Timeouts and retries': ['A timeout limits waiting, not necessarily execution. A retry starts another attempt, which may duplicate an earlier operation whose response was lost.', 'caller has 300 ms budget | each attempt and backoff spends remaining time | stop admitting attempts when budget expires', 'Does a timeout prove the server did nothing? No. It may have committed before the response was lost. Use idempotency or reconciliation before retrying writes.', 'Use a controlled clock and dependency delay. Verify no new attempt begins after the deadline; separately test whether underlying work actually supports cancellation.'],
  'Circuit breakers': ['A breaker tracks outcomes and temporarily refuses calls after a failure threshold. Half-open probes decide whether traffic can resume.', 'closed accepts calls | threshold opens breaker | limited probe either closes or reopens', 'Is a circuit breaker a concurrency limiter? No. A breaker reacts to health outcomes; a bulkhead limits simultaneous work. Many slow calls can accumulate before a breaker opens.', 'Drive failures with a controlled clock, verify open calls fail fast, then allow one probe. Test both successful recovery and a failed probe reopening.'],
  'Event-driven design': ['An event records a fact that happened. Consumers react later, so duplicate delivery, ordering and recovery must be part of the contract.', 'commit order and outbox together | relay publishes durable fact | consumer deduplicates and records progress', 'Does an outbox guarantee exactly one consumer effect? No. Relay and consumer retries can duplicate delivery. Couple effect and consumer progress where possible and reconcile external effects.', 'Deliver one event twice and crash between processing and acknowledgement. Verify the chosen deduplication policy preserves the intended effect after replay.'],
  'Big-O reasoning': ['Complexity describes growth with input size, not an exact runtime prediction. State what n means and count the operation that dominates.', 'scan values from start | compare until found or exhausted | at most n comparisons and constant extra state', 'Can O(n) beat O(log n)? For small input, constants and memory locality can dominate. Big-O describes scaling under stated assumptions rather than a universal speed ranking.', 'Search empty input, a first-element hit and a missing target. Comparisons are 0, 1 and n respectively; worst-case time remains O(n).'],
  'Two pointers': ['Two positions move through sorted values. Ordering proves which candidates can be discarded without losing a solution.', 'compare left plus right | sum too small advances left | sum too large retreats right', 'Why require sorted input? Without order, moving a pointer cannot prove the discarded pairs are impossible. Sort first or use another algorithm and include its cost.', 'Use [-3,0,3] with target 0: true. Use two maximum ints with target -2: false, because summing in long prevents a false match from overflow.'],
  'Sliding window': ['A window is a contiguous input range. Grow its right side, then shrink the left until a maintained constraint becomes valid again.', 'ece grows within two distinct chars | adding b breaks constraint | shrink until valid and retain best length 3', 'Does every sum problem permit this technique? No. Negative numbers can break the monotonic validity rule used by positive-sum windows. Prove the rule for the actual constraint.', 'For eceba the longest at-most-two-distinct substring is ece, length 3. Empty input returns 0; aa returns 2.'],
  'Binary search': ['Binary search discards half an ordered interval using a monotonic yes-or-no rule. The example finds the first value at least the target.', 'start interval [0,length) | compare midpoint with target | shrink to first valid boundary or length', 'Why return length for no match? It is the insertion boundary after all values. The caller must check it before indexing; this is not an exact-match return contract.', 'For [2,2,2] and target 2 return 0. For [1,3] and target 4 return 2. Empty input returns 0 without indexing.'],
  'Recursion': ['A recursive method solves a smaller version of its own problem. A base case stops calls and a decreasing measure proves eventual termination.', 'factorial 5 calls factorial 4 | descend to base case 1 | multiply while returning to obtain 120', 'Is recursion free because the code is short? No. Calls consume stack depth; unbounded input can exhaust the stack. Use iteration or an explicit stack when needed.', 'factorial 0 returns 1. Reject -1 and 13 for this int implementation; 13 factorial exceeds the supported range even though recursion would terminate.'],
  'Dynamic programming': ['Store subproblem results when the same smaller problems recur. Define each table entry and the dependencies needed before filling it.', 'fib 0 and fib 1 are base cases | each next entry sums previous two | fib 46 is 1836311903', 'When may I reduce memory? When future transitions need only a bounded recent subset. Fibonacci needs two prior values, but reconstruction problems may need more history.', 'Check 0 returns 0 and 1 returns 1. Reject 47 because int would overflow. Comparing with naive recursion at small n checks the recurrence independently.'],
  'Graph traversal': ['A graph has vertices and edges. Breadth-first search uses a queue to visit reachable vertices in increasing unweighted distance from the start.', 'enqueue vertex 0 and mark seen | remove a vertex and enqueue unseen neighbors | stop when queue is empty', 'Why mark visited on enqueue? Marking later allows multiple parents to enqueue the same vertex, increasing work and making traversal reasoning harder.', 'Use edges 0 to 1, 1 to 0, and a disconnected vertex 2. Starting at 0 must visit exactly 0 and 1 and terminate despite the cycle.'],
  'Topological sorting': ['A dependency order is possible only when directed dependencies contain no cycle. Indegree counts unfinished prerequisites.', 'enqueue zero-indegree vertices | emit one and decrement dependents | emit all vertices or report a cycle', 'Is a partial result valid when a cycle exists? No. It orders only part of the graph. Check emitted count against vertex count and report the unschedulable cycle.', 'For A to B, A must precede B. Add B to A: no complete order exists. A disconnected C can appear anywhere consistent with the other dependencies.'],
  'Union-find': ['Disjoint-set union tracks which elements belong to the same component. A representative identifies each component; union joins two components.', 'elements 0 and 1 start separate | union joins representatives | connected 0 1 becomes true', 'Can it find shortest paths? No. It stores component membership, not the sequence of edges between vertices. Use a graph traversal for paths.', 'Union 0 and 1 twice: component count decreases only once. Vertex 2 stays disconnected; validate indexes before accessing parent storage.'],
  'Range queries': ['A prefix array stores totals before each boundary. Subtracting two prefix totals isolates a half-open range without scanning it again.', 'prefix is 0 3 8 10 | range [1,3) uses prefix 3 minus prefix 1 | result is 7', 'Can I keep prefix sums after changing the source? Not without updating affected prefixes. For many updates, consider a Fenwick or segment tree and its extra complexity.', 'Range [0,0) is empty and sums to 0. Range [0,3) is 10. Check boundaries before indexing and use long for totals that may exceed int.'],
};

export function chapterTeaching(title) {
  const entry = notes[title];
  if (!entry) throw new Error(`Missing authored Java teaching: ${title}`);
  const [beginner, trace, followUp, solution] = entry;
  return { beginner, traceSteps: trace.split(' | '), followUp, solution };
}
